===Etymology===
{{wp|ja:}}
{{ja-kanjitab|みなと|yomi=k}}
From {{inh|ja|ojp|-|sort=みなと}}. Attested in the ''{{w|Nihon Shoki}}'' of 720.<ref name="JDB">{{R:JDB|page=713}}</ref><ref name="KDJ2O"/> Reconstructed as deriving from {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|*minato|sort=みなと}}.

The traditional etymology that Japanese sources trace back to is a compound of {{com|ja|水|tr1=mi-|t1=water|な|tr2=na|pos2=assimilated [[apophony|apophonic]] form of {{m|ja|の|tr=no|t=genitive case marker}}|門|tr3=to|t3=gate}}.<ref name="KDJ2O"/><ref name="DJR"/><ref name="DDJS">{{R:Digital-Daijisen|港}}</ref><ref name="K">{{R:Kojien}}</ref><ref name="SMK5"/> 
* However, this does not correlate cleanly with the etymology of {{m|ja|水|tr=mizu|t=water}}, reconstructed as {{m+|jpx-pro|*mentu|t=water|w=1}}.  Then again, there are numerous attested words where {{m|ja|水|tr=mi}} is used as the first and last element in compounds, suggesting either that the derivation of {{m|ja|水|tr=mizu}} might differ.<!--, or that the final ''-zu'' component was subject to [[elision]].-->

Vovin, on the other hand suggests that the initial ''mi-'' was {{m|ja|御|tr=mi-|t=honorific prefix}}, while ''-na-'' meant "water", possibly from {{m+|tai-pro|*C̬.namꟲ|t=water|w=1}}.<ref name="Vovin">{{cite-journal|ja|last=Vovin|first=Alexander|title=上代日本語と古代・中世韓国語の「水」と「涙」|journal=日韓言語学者会議|publisher=麗澤大学言語研究センター|year=2010|pages=115-120}}</ref> Compare {{m|ja|涙|tr=namida|t=tears}}, {{m|ja|菜葱}}, {{m|ja|水葱|tr=nagi|t={{taxlink|Pontederia vaginalis|species|Monochoria vaginalis}}}}, {{m|ja|漬く|tr=nazuku|t=soak in water|pos=obsolete}}.
* However, this may present semantic difficulties, as any native formation like ''namida'' that proposes "water" for the initial ''nam'' and "eye" for a following component reverses the usual word-formation pattern for Japanese, where the main or head noun comes last.  In addition, the ''na'' element appears in other words with no relation to "water", as an assimilated apophonic form of genitive particle {{ja-r|の}}, seen in terms such as {{m|ja|掌|tr=tanagokoro|lit=hand's heart/center||palm of the hand}}, {{m|ja|眼間|tr=manakai|lit=eyes' exchanging/crossing||where the lines of sight of the eyes converge}}.

