The two pronunciations have the same etymology but later split into two different pronunciations, with pronunciation 2 becoming specific for place names.

{{glossary|Folk etymology}} relates the second pronunciation to a legend, in which {{w|Qianlong Emperor}} mispronounced this character using the {{l|zh|有邊讀邊}} when he visited Xushuguan. As a result, people followed the pronunciation by the emperor.

